---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "../../components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import TrustBlock from "../../components/TrustBlock.astro";
import { editorialTeam, carTrustPoints } from "../../data/editorial";

const title = "Mazda 3 2020: what to avoid";
const description =
  "What to avoid on a Mazda 3 2020, including front-end clunks, brake noise, road hum, and short-trip battery complaints.";
const intro =
  "The 2020 Mazda 3 feels upscale for the class, so small refinement problems stand out quickly. Avoid examples where the seller leans on the premium feel while ignoring front-end clunks, brake squeal, road hum, or weak battery behavior.";
const modifiedTime = new Date("2026-05-13");
const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: "Home", href: "/" },
  { label: "Guides", href: "/guides/" },
  { label: title }
];

const relatedProblems = [
  { href: "/problems/front-end-clunk-over-bumps-mazda-3-2020/", title: "Front-end clunk over bumps on Mazda 3 2020", description: "Best when the suspension noise is already visible on the test drive.", eyebrow: "Problem guide" },
  { href: "/problems/brake-squeal-at-low-speed-mazda-3-2020/", title: "Brake squeal at low speed on Mazda 3 2020", description: "Useful when the noise is being dismissed as harmless without inspection.", eyebrow: "Problem guide" },
  { href: "/problems/humming-noise-that-gets-louder-with-speed/", title: "Humming noise that gets louder with speed", description: "Open this before accepting a generic tire-noise explanation.", eyebrow: "Problem guide" }
];

const relatedBest = [
  { href: "/best/best-front-sway-bar-links-for-mazda-3-2020/", title: "Best front sway bar links for Mazda 3 2020", description: "Best after the front-end clunk points toward sway bar link wear.", eyebrow: "Sway Bar Links" },
  { href: "/best/best-brake-pads-for-mazda-3-2020/", title: "Best brake pads for Mazda 3 2020", description: "Best when brake squeal is confirmed and the car is still worth buying.", eyebrow: "Brake Pads" }
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  intro={intro}
  image="/images/photos/cars/mazda-3-2020.webp"
  modifiedTime={modifiedTime}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  editorialSummary="The focus is on Mazda 3 examples where refinement issues are already visible before purchase."
  editorialByline={`By ${editorialTeam.name}`}
  editorialReviewedLabel={`Reviewed ${modifiedTime.toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "short", day: "numeric", year: "numeric" })}`}
  editorialBadges={["Avoid guide", "Model-year specific", "Buying-focused"]}
>
  <TrustBlock title="Best starting point" summary="Start here when a Mazda 3 looks clean, but the noise and refinement checks tell a less polished story." byline={`By ${editorialTeam.name}`} checkedLabel={`Checked ${modifiedTime.toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "short", day: "numeric", year: "numeric" })}`} points={carTrustPoints} />

  <Section title="The short version" intro="These are the Mazda 3 examples to avoid first.">
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Avoid obvious front-end clunks over small bumps unless the price already reflects diagnosis and repair.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Avoid cars with brake squeal plus rough pedal feel, vibration, or neglected hardware.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Avoid road hum that changes with speed until tires and wheel-end condition are checked separately.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Walk-away patterns" intro="The risky examples usually have small symptoms stacked together.">
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Front-end clunk is present on both rough pavement and driveway entries.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Brake noise is paired with visible rotor wear, vibration, or uneven feel.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">The seller blames every hum on tires without rotation history or tread inspection.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Short-trip battery weakness appears together with other deferred maintenance.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="What is still negotiable" intro="Some issues are manageable when the car is otherwise clean and priced honestly.">
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">A narrow sway-bar-link noise with no larger steering or suspension symptoms.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Brake squeal that still looks like pad compound, glazing, or hardware service.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Road noise that follows tire wear and improves after rotation or tire replacement.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Inspection order" intro="Run these checks in order so the test drive separates normal compact-car noise from a car that is already asking for repairs.">
    <ol class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Start with slow uneven pavement, parking-lot turns, and driveway angles. A single dull clunk can be negotiable; repeated sharp noises or steering feel changes deserve a deeper suspension inspection.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Then do several normal stops from city speed. Brake noise by itself is not the whole story, so listen for grinding and feel for vibration before accepting a simple pad explanation.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Finish with a steady highway-speed run. If the hum changes with speed more than road surface, check tire wear and wheel-end play before deciding the car is just louder than expected.</li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section title="How to price the risk" intro="A clean Mazda 3 should feel tight enough that small issues are easy to isolate. The price should move when they are not.">
    <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      Do not treat a premium-feeling cabin as proof that the example has been maintained well. If two or more refinement complaints show up on the same drive, price the car like it needs diagnosis, not just cleaning. The safer buy is the Mazda 3 with boring records, consistent tire wear, predictable stops, and one explainable issue at most.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Final decision checkpoint" intro="Best before deciding whether the example is a smart buy or simply a clean-looking car with deferred work.">
    <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      A good 2020 Mazda 3 should not need you to make excuses for every noise. One narrow issue can be fine if the seller has records and the repair path is obvious. Multiple vague issues are different: clunk, squeal, hum, and weak battery behavior together mean the first month will be spent diagnosing the car instead of enjoying it. In that case, either negotiate enough room for a proper inspection and repair sequence or move to a cleaner example.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="What to do next" intro="Let the strongest symptom decide the next page and the negotiation.">
    <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      Use the clunk guide when suspension noise is the issue, the Mazda 3 brake-squeal guide when stopping noise is the issue, and the road-hum guide when speed-linked noise is the issue. Keeping those paths separate protects you from replacing parts just because the car feels less polished than it should.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <RelatedLinks title="Related problem guides" intro="Open these when the Mazda 3 already shows one of the symptoms above." items={relatedProblems} />
  <RelatedLinks title="Related best-parts guides" intro="Best when the car is still worth buying but front-end wear needs a realistic parts path." items={relatedBest} />
</ContentLayout>
